#: ../../get_start/weighted_fields/weighted_fields.rst:2
msgid "Weighted, marked, and derived fields"
msgstr ""

#: ../../get_start/weighted_fields/weighted_fields.rst:4
msgid ""
"The field--window language is not limited to tracer counts. Catalogue "
"weights and particle-carried values change *what field is represented*; "
"windows change *what operation is applied to that field*. The same "
"``SFCProjection`` and ``WindowFunc`` objects can therefore construct "
"marked density fields, velocity and momentum fields, differential "
"operators, the Newtonian potential, and acceleration."
msgstr ""

#: ../../get_start/weighted_fields/weighted_fields.rst:11
msgid ""
"The executable companion is ``examples/notebooks/weighted_fields.ipynb``."
" It keeps the field construction compact and spends most of its length on"
" slice selection and publication-quality visualisation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../get_start/weighted_fields/weighted_fields.rst:16
msgid "Catalogue weight versus field value"
msgstr ""

#: ../../get_start/weighted_fields/weighted_fields.rst:18
#, python-brace-format
msgid "For particle positions :math:`\\mathbf{x}_i`, PyHermes projects"
msgstr ""

#: ../../get_start/weighted_fields/weighted_fields.rst:20
msgid ""
"F_q(\\mathbf{x})=\n"
"\\sum_i {w_i^{\\rm cat}q_i\\over Z}\n"
"\\delta_{\\rm D}^{(3)}(\\mathbf{x}-\\mathbf{x}_i)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../get_start/weighted_fields/weighted_fields.rst:26
msgid ""
"``catalog_weight`` holds completeness, selection, or other catalogue "
"weights. ``field_value`` holds the quantity carried by each object: a "
"mark, mass, or one velocity component. Keeping these roles separate "
"prevents a signed physical quantity from being mistaken for a "
"normalization weight."
msgstr ""

#: ../../get_start/weighted_fields/weighted_fields.rst:31
msgid "``weight_normalization`` chooses :math:`Z`:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../get_start/weighted_fields/weighted_fields.rst:33
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"``catalog`` divides by :math:`\\sum_i w_i^{\\rm cat}` and is the default "
"for normalized clustering fields."
msgstr ""

#: ../../get_start/weighted_fields/weighted_fields.rst:35
msgid ""
"``raw`` uses :math:`Z=1`; choose it when the absolute physical amplitude "
"is required."
msgstr ""

#: ../../get_start/weighted_fields/weighted_fields.rst:37
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"``field`` divides by :math:`\\sum_i w_i^{\\rm cat}q_i` and is appropriate"
" only when that field sum is meaningful and non-zero."
msgstr ""

#: ../../get_start/weighted_fields/weighted_fields.rst:40
msgid "One small helper can build a family of fields on identical MRA metadata:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../get_start/weighted_fields/weighted_fields.rst:66
msgid "Velocity as a ratio of fields"
msgstr ""

#: ../../get_start/weighted_fields/weighted_fields.rst:68
msgid ""
"A velocity-weighted field is not yet a volume-weighted velocity. After "
"applying the same smoothing window to numerator and denominator,"
msgstr ""

#: ../../get_start/weighted_fields/weighted_fields.rst:71
msgid "v_i(\\mathbf{x})={n_{v_i}(\\mathbf{x})\\over n(\\mathbf{x})}."
msgstr ""

#: ../../get_start/weighted_fields/weighted_fields.rst:75
msgid "The derivative must obey the quotient rule:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../get_start/weighted_fields/weighted_fields.rst:77
msgid ""
"\\partial_j v_i=\n"
"{\\partial_j n_{v_i}\\over n}\n"
"-{n_{v_i}\\,\\partial_j n\\over n^2}."
msgstr ""

#: ../../get_start/weighted_fields/weighted_fields.rst:83
msgid ""
"This distinction matters in sparse regions. Mask points where the "
"smoothed count field is too small instead of allowing a tiny denominator "
"to manufacture large velocities."
msgstr ""

#: ../../get_start/weighted_fields/weighted_fields.rst:88
msgid "Differential windows"
msgstr ""

#: ../../get_start/weighted_fields/weighted_fields.rst:90
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"With the package Fourier convention, a directional derivative along the "
"unit vector :math:`\\widehat{\\mathbf n}` is the multiplier"
msgstr ""

#: ../../get_start/weighted_fields/weighted_fields.rst:93
msgid ""
"\\widehat W_{\\partial_{\\widehat n}}(\\mathbf{k})\n"
"=2\\pi i(\\mathbf{k}\\cdot\\widehat{\\mathbf n})."
msgstr ""

#: ../../get_start/weighted_fields/weighted_fields.rst:98
msgid ""
"Three such windows give gradients; component combinations give divergence"
" and curl. The Laplacian window applies :math:`-(2\\pi k)^2`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../get_start/weighted_fields/weighted_fields.rst:121
msgid ""
"Window derivatives are formed in grid coordinates. Multiply sampled "
"values by ``field.scale_factor`` when converting one derivative to "
"inverse physical-box units. This is why the tutorial keeps coordinate "
"conversion adjacent to the sampling code rather than hiding it inside the"
" mathematical window."
msgstr ""

#: ../../get_start/weighted_fields/weighted_fields.rst:127
msgid "Potential from the inverse Laplacian"
msgstr ""

#: ../../get_start/weighted_fields/weighted_fields.rst:129
msgid ""
"The built-in ``inverse_laplacian`` is deliberately a pure mathematical "
"operator:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../get_start/weighted_fields/weighted_fields.rst:132
msgid ""
"\\widehat W_{\\nabla^{-2}}(\\mathbf{k})=\n"
"-{1\\over(2\\pi k)^2},\\qquad \\widehat W_{\\nabla^{-2}}(0)=0."
msgstr ""

#: ../../get_start/weighted_fields/weighted_fields.rst:137
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"Removing the zero mode fixes the arbitrary additive constant of the "
"potential; it does not discard a measurable force. For the matter "
"contrast :math:`\\delta_{\\rm m}` in comoving coordinates,"
msgstr ""

#: ../../get_start/weighted_fields/weighted_fields.rst:141
msgid ""
"{\\Phi\\over c^2}\n"
"={3\\Omega_{\\rm m0}H_0^2\\over2ac^2}\n"
"\\nabla^{-2}\\delta_{\\rm m}."
msgstr ""

#: ../../get_start/weighted_fields/weighted_fields.rst:147
msgid ""
"Keeping the cosmological prefactor outside ``WindowFunc`` makes units and"
" coordinate conventions explicit. If the box coordinates are in "
":math:`h^{-1}\\mathrm{Mpc}`, use "
":math:`H_0=100\\,\\mathrm{km\\,s^{-1}}/(h^{-1}\\mathrm{Mpc})`; the "
"numerical :math:`h` is already carried by the coordinate unit. The "
"notebook also multiplies the grid-space inverse Laplacian by the squared "
"cell size."
msgstr ""

#: ../../get_start/weighted_fields/weighted_fields.rst:169
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"The resulting :math:`\\Phi/c^2` is dimensionless and mean-zero. Its "
"broad, smooth appearance is physical: the :math:`k^{-2}` response "
"strongly emphasizes large scales."
msgstr ""

#: ../../get_start/weighted_fields/weighted_fields.rst:174
msgid "Acceleration and linear flow"
msgstr ""

#: ../../get_start/weighted_fields/weighted_fields.rst:176
msgid "Directional derivatives of the potential give the peculiar acceleration,"
msgstr ""

#: ../../get_start/weighted_fields/weighted_fields.rst:178
#, python-brace-format
msgid "\\mathbf{g}=-{c^2\\over a}\\nabla_x(\\Phi/c^2)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../get_start/weighted_fields/weighted_fields.rst:182
msgid ""
"In linear theory the matter contrast, velocity divergence, potential, and"
" acceleration are linked by the continuity and Poisson equations. After "
"matching units and smoothing scales, their large-scale patterns should "
"correlate; exact point-by-point equality is not expected for halo "
"velocities because of bias, nonlinear motions, sparse sampling, and "
"reconstruction error."
msgstr ""

#: ../../get_start/weighted_fields/weighted_fields.rst:188
msgid "Velocity divergence, matter contrast, potential, and acceleration"
msgstr ""

#: ../../get_start/weighted_fields/weighted_fields.rst:193
msgid ""
"Halo velocity divergence, smoothed matter contrast, and the Newtonian "
"potential on a common slice. Velocity and acceleration arrows expose the "
"large-scale flow rather than merely the scalar amplitudes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../get_start/weighted_fields/weighted_fields.rst:197
msgid "Linear-theory comparisons for velocity divergence and acceleration"
msgstr ""

#: ../../get_start/weighted_fields/weighted_fields.rst:202
msgid ""
"Linear-theory checks are most informative after using the same smoothing "
"scale and evaluating both fields at matched positions."
msgstr ""

#: ../../get_start/weighted_fields/weighted_fields.rst:206
msgid "Marked 2PCFs"
msgstr ""

#: ../../get_start/weighted_fields/weighted_fields.rst:208
msgid ""
"A mark is simply another ``field_value``. A common choice derives it from"
" a local smoothed density, for example"
msgstr ""

#: ../../get_start/weighted_fields/weighted_fields.rst:211
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"m_{\\rm power}=(\\rho_R+\\epsilon)^\\alpha,\n"
"\\qquad\n"
"m_{\\rm inverse}=\\left({\\rho_*+1\\over\\rho_*+\\rho_R}\\right)^p."
msgstr ""

#: ../../get_start/weighted_fields/weighted_fields.rst:217
msgid ""
"Evaluate :math:`\\rho_R` at catalogue positions, normalize the marks by "
"their catalogue-weighted mean, project the marked catalogue, and pass "
"that ``SFCField`` to the unchanged ``Corr_2PCF`` task. No marked-pair "
"estimator is needed: the mark changes the field, not the binning "
"operation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../get_start/weighted_fields/weighted_fields.rst:222
msgid "Marked two-point correlation functions"
msgstr ""

#: ../../get_start/weighted_fields/weighted_fields.rst:227
msgid ""
"Power-law and inverse-density marks emphasize different environments "
"while reusing the same 2PCF field--window estimator."
msgstr ""

#: ../../get_start/weighted_fields/weighted_fields.rst:231
msgid "Scope and interpretation"
msgstr ""

#: ../../get_start/weighted_fields/weighted_fields.rst:233
msgid ""
"These operator windows make many derived fields concise, but they do not "
"make all of them automatically physical. Always state the catalogue "
"normalization, coordinate units, smoothing scale, zero-mode convention, "
"and whether the field is tracer- or matter-based. The algebra is short; "
"the assumptions still deserve their full names."
msgstr ""
